# Transformer Language Model

This project implements a small Transformer language model from scratch in PyTorch.

The project focuses on understanding the internal behavior of Transformer components through implementation detail and ablation experiments rather than only achieving the best validation loss.

## Background

The initial implementation was developed as part of the CS336 coursework. I used the tests provided by the course to verify the correctness of my implementation of the main Transformer components.

After completing the core implementation, I extended the project to investigate training behavior and the effects of different design choices.

## Project Goals
* Implement a Transformer language model from scratch in PyTorch.
* Understand the mathematical and implementation details behind each component.
* Study how initialization and normalization choices affect training.
* Perform controlled ablation experiments and analyze their effects on model behavior.

## Implements Features

* Transformer language model
* Multi-Head Self-Attention
* Rotary Positional Embeddings (RoPE)
* RMSNorm
* SwiGLU feed-forward network
* Residual connections
* AdamW optimizer
* Cross-entropy loss
* Autoregressive text generation
* Temperature and Top-p sampling
* Training and validation loss tracking
* Model checkpointing
* Hyperparameter experiments

## Project Structure

```text
.
├── src/ # Model and generation
├── configs/ # Training configurations
├── experiments/ # Ablation experiment results 
├── checkpoints/ # Saved model checkpoints
├── hyperparameter_sweep/ # hyperparameter sweep results
├── tokenizer/ # tokenizer
├── training/ # training implementation, load checkpoint
├── README.md
└── pyproject.toml
```

## Training

A training run can be configured with parameters such as:

* learning rate
* batch size
* context length
* number of layers
* model dimension
* number of attention heads
* feed-forward dimension
* number of training steps

Training records training and validation loss during the run and periodically saves model checkpoints.

## Hyperparameter Sweep

I performed learning-rate sweeps to identify a reasonable training configuration.

The sweep was conducted in multiple stages. An initial coarse search tested several learning rates, followed by a finer search around the best-performing region.

For example:

Initial search:
0.001, 0.002, 0.004, 0.006

Refined search:
0.0007, 0.0009, 0.0010, 0.0012, 0.0015

The refined sweep identified 0.0012 as the best-performing max_learning rate among the tested configurations. This value was then used for the subsequent experiments.

## Experiments
### RMSNorm Scale Ablation

I compare:

Baseline: RMSNorm with a learnable per-channel scale parameter (γ)
No-weight: RMSNorm without the learnable scale parameter

Both models use the same architecture and training setup except for the RMSNorm scale parameters.

I evaluate:

* Training and validation loss
* RMSNorm scale statistics
* Activation RMS across layers
* Attention parameter statistics
* SwiGLU parameter statistics
* Performance during continued training

The experiment uses three random seeds and training runs of up to 6000 steps, followed by an additional continuation experiment from step 6000 to 9000.
#### Key Findings

The RMSNorm ablation shows that removing the learnable scale parameter does not prevent the Transformer from training. Check out the *experiments/Ablation: Removing the Learnable Weight from RMSNorm.md* for more details

## Checkpoints

Model checkpoints are saved at selected training iterations.

For experiments where I only need to analyze the model, the checkpoint only contains the model parameters; It will save both model status as well as optimizer status on the last iteration of training, so that I can continue training from specifc checkpoint. *load_checkingPoint.py* implements this feature

For checkpoint saving, make sure to give unique saving directory so that checkpoint wouldn't overwrite.

*load_ckp.py* has other implementation that i used for my ablation experiement to get parameter's statistics and model activation(make sure to change model implementation accordingly when running baseline/ablation model for activation).

## Running the Project

Configure the experiment parameters in the corresponding configuration file, install required dependencies, set up a virtual environment and run the training script:
```bash
    uv run python -m training/train.py
```

